1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,801
Kevin runs a mobile dog grooming business. His clients currently
text to book, and requests

2
00:00:04,801 --> 00:00:09,602
get lost. This working MVP replaces that scattered process with a
clear appointment request form

3
00:00:09,602 --> 00:00:14,403
and a persistent workspace for Kevin.

4
00:00:15,503 --> 00:00:19,809
A client starts here. They enter their dog’s name, breed and size,
choose a service,

5
00:00:19,809 --> 00:00:24,114
and suggest a date and time window. The page works on a phone, so
clients

6
00:00:24,114 --> 00:00:28,420
can request a visit wherever they are.

7
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:34,062
The form also collects a contact number, the service address, and
optional care notes. In

8
00:00:34,062 --> 00:00:38,605
this example, Taylor is requesting a full groom for Teddy. Required
fields and dates are

9
00:00:38,605 --> 00:00:43,147
checked before anything is saved.

10
00:00:44,247 --> 00:00:48,765
After submission, the client receives a reference and a clear next
step. This is a

11
00:00:48,765 --> 00:00:53,284
request, not an automatic booking. Kevin will contact the client to
agree on availability, the

12
00:00:53,284 --> 00:00:57,802
price, and the exact time.

13
00:00:58,902 --> 00:01:04,015
Back in Kevin’s workspace, Teddy’s request appears automatically.
The waiting count and unread badge update.

14
00:01:04,015 --> 00:01:09,127
The open workspace checks for new requests every eight seconds and
shows an in-page alert.

15
00:01:09,127 --> 00:01:14,240
Browser alerts are optional when permission is granted.

16
00:01:15,340 --> 00:01:20,016
Opening the request shows the dog, service, preferred visit,
address, contact links, and care notes

17
00:01:20,016 --> 00:01:24,692
in one place. Kevin can search and filter the inbox instead of
searching through separate

18
00:01:24,692 --> 00:01:29,368
text conversations.

19
00:01:30,468 --> 00:01:34,851
After agreeing the visit with the client, Kevin records the
confirmed time. The request moves

20
00:01:34,851 --> 00:01:39,234
out of the waiting list. When the grooming is finished, he marks the
visit completed,

21
00:01:39,234 --> 00:01:43,617
keeping a useful history of the work.

22
00:01:44,717 --> 00:01:51,161
Bookings are stored in a database and survive reloads. Real client
details are protected behind

23
00:01:51,161 --> 00:01:57,604
owner access. Reviewers can use a separate practice workspace with
fictional data. This MVP keeps

24
00:01:57,604 --> 00:02:04,048
Kevin in control: no automatic payments or messages, and
notifications require an open workspace.
